.TH HOGAR-SHELL 1 "" "hogar-shell 0.1.0" "hogar-shell"
.SH NAME
hogar-shell \- a Wayland desktop shell for Hyprland
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hogar-shell
[run]
.br
.B hogar-shell
<target> <cmd> [args]
.br
.B hogar-shell
toggle <module>
.br
.B hogar-shell
launcher
.br
.B hogar-shell
\-\-list
.br
.B hogar-shell
config schema [name]
.br
.B hogar-shell
config check
.br
.B hogar-shell
man commands|config
.br
.B hogar-shell
\-\-help | \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
hogar-shell is a Wayland desktop shell: bars, panels, a launcher, a dashboard, a lock screen,
notifications, screen capture and wallpaper\-derived theming, configured in TOML.
.PP
Started with no arguments it runs the shell. With arguments it is a client: the request is sent to the
running shell over a Unix socket and the reply printed, so every action the shell has is available to a
compositor keybind or a script. The exit status mirrors the reply, so a caller can tell a refused
command from one that worked without parsing prose.
.PP
The shell also registers its actions as XDG global shortcuts, which can be bound through the desktop
portal instead of by command.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help, \-h
print the usage block and exit
.TP
.B \-\-version, \-V
print the version and exit
.TP
.B \-\-list, \-s
print every target, command and argument the shell answers
.PP
.BR "config schema" ,
.BR "config check" ,
.B deps
and
.B man
are answered by the binary rather than sent to the shell: each is a function of this build, this
machine and the files on it, and the case a dependency report or a config check is for is the one
where nothing started.
.SH COMMANDS
A request is a target, a command and its arguments.
.BR hogar-shell (5)
documents the configuration file.
